course: adult-life
lesson: 8
title: Borrowing and debt
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Debt is money that has been borrowed and is still owed. Most adults have some, like a mortgage or a car loan, and planned debt can be fine.
explain: Borrowing always has a price called interest, so the total paid back is more than the price tag. Debt becomes a problem when the repayments are more than the family can afford.
explain: Lenders make borrowing look easy: "Cash in minutes!", "Buy now, pay later!". The easiest money is usually the most expensive kind.
explain: Example: in a flat in Dublin, a letter arrives with red writing across the top: "Final demand." Dad goes pale, then picks up the phone and rings a free debt charity. By the end of the call there is a plan, and he says, "Right. We can do this."
explain: Debt is common and can be sorted. Sorting it is the grown-ups' job, not the child's, and asking for help early is the sensible move.
fact: Debt is money borrowed and still owed; it becomes a problem when repayments are more than the family can afford.
fact: A credit card bill shows what was spent that month; paying all of it means no interest, paying part makes the debt grow.
fact: The minimum payment barely covers the interest, so paying only the minimum keeps a debt going for years.
fact: A loan means borrowing a set amount and paying it back in fixed amounts plus interest, so the total cost is more than the price.
fact: Interest is the price of using someone else's money, and a small charge every year adds up to a lot over many years.
fact: A payday loan is a small short-term loan with very high interest, and people can get trapped borrowing again to repay it.
fact: Buy now pay later splits a purchase into instalments; it is still borrowing, with charges if a payment is late.
fact: A credit score is a rating of how reliably someone pays; missing payments lowers it and makes borrowing harder and dearer.
fact: A final demand is a warning that a bill is very overdue and stronger action will follow, so acting early is easier.
fact: Bailiffs can collect money owed only after a court decision and many warnings, under strict rules, and cannot take essentials.
fact: Debt advice charities give free, private help to make a plan for paying debts back at a pace the family can manage.
fact: Lending money to friends can cause arguments, so many grown-ups only lend what they could afford to lose.
exercise: Dad says "We're in a bit of debt." What does it mean? | The family has borrowed money and still owes it | The family has saved a lot of money in a special account | The family has won some money | The family has paid everything off
exercise: When does debt become a real problem? | When the repayments are more than the family can afford | Whenever a family has any debt at all, even a very small one | Only when the debt is for a house | Only when the bank sends a birthday card
exercise: Mum says "The credit card bill's arrived." How can she avoid paying interest? | Pay the whole bill on time | Pay only the minimum each month | Hide the bill until next year | Buy more things on the card
exercise: Grandad warns "Pay only the minimum and you'll be paying it off forever." Why? | The minimum barely covers the interest | The minimum is double the whole bill, so it hurts | The bank stops interest after the minimum | The minimum goes to charity, not the debt
exercise: "We took out a loan for the car." What will the car really cost? | More than the price tag, because of interest | Exactly the price tag, no more and no less | Less than the price, because loans come with a discount | Nothing, because the bank pays for it
exercise: Mum says "That's how they get you, with the interest." What does she mean? | Interest makes borrowed money cost much more in the end | Interest is a free gift from the lender to say thank you | Interest is only charged on savings | Interest is paid by the shop, not you
exercise: An advert on TV shouts "Cash in your account in minutes!" What is it usually? | A very expensive short-term loan | A free gift from the government to help with bills | A prize you have already won | Your own savings coming back
exercise: "Buy now, pay later, it's interest-free!" What is true about it? | It is still borrowing, with charges if you pay late | It is a free gift that never ever needs paying back | It means the shop pays the bill for you | It only works for food and bills
exercise: Dad says "My credit score has dropped." What follows? | Borrowing becomes harder and more expensive | He gets a bigger loan more easily from any bank | His pay goes down at work | His bank account is closed at once
exercise: How does someone build up a good credit score? | By paying bills and debts on time | By borrowing as much as possible | By never opening letters from the bank | By paying only the minimum
exercise: A letter in red says "Final demand." What is it? | A warning that a bill is very overdue | A thank-you letter for paying the bill early | A prize draw from the bank | The first bill ever for a new phone
exercise: Neighbours whisper "The bailiffs came round next door." What are bailiffs? | Agents sent after a court decision to collect money owed | Police officers who arrest people with debts and take them to prison | Builders who repair houses for free | Friends who lend money to neighbours
exercise: Someone knocks and says "I'm a bailiff, let me in." You are alone. What do you do? | Don't open the door and tell a grown-up | Open the door and let them look round the house | Hand over your games console | Tell them where the money is kept
exercise: Mum says "I've rung the debt charity about the bills." What will the charity do? | Give free private help to make a repayment plan | Lend the family more money at a very high interest | Tell the neighbours about the debt | Take the family's things to sell
exercise: Uncle asks "Can you lend me a tenner till Friday?" What rule do many grown-ups follow? | Only lend what you could afford to lose | Always lend whatever anyone asks, however much | Never lend to family, only to strangers | Lend only if they promise in writing
exercise: You see a scary letter about money on the table. What should you do? | Tell a calm grown-up rather than worry in secret | Hide it under the sofa so that nobody gets upset | Throw it in the bin quickly | Ring the company yourself to explain
test: Your brother says "Debt means we're bad people." Put him right. | Debt is common, most adults have some, and it can be sorted | He is right, only bad people ever borrow money from a bank | Debt means the family will lose everything | Debt means the bank owns the children
test: Overheard on the phone: "Look, I've only paid the minimum for two years and it's barely gone down!" Why? | The minimum mostly paid the interest, not the debt | The bank forgot to take his payments for two whole years | Minimum payments cancel each other out | The debt grows only on weekends
test: Your cousin wants a £300 bike "on buy now pay later, it's basically free". What is the best question to ask? | Could you afford it today, and what if a payment is late? | Which colour bike do you want? | Can you get two bikes on the plan? | Will the bike be delivered in minutes?
test: Dad grumbles "Missed one phone payment and now the flat agency won't rent to us." What has probably happened? | His credit score dropped from the missed payment | Phone companies own all the flats in the town | The agency does not like his phone | Missing payments raises the score
test: Mum: "Stop hiding the letters, Steve, the longer you leave it the worse it gets." What is true? | Acting early on overdue bills is much easier | Ignoring the letters for long enough makes debts disappear | Hidden letters cannot be legally sent | Letters about money are always mistakes
test: A man on telly says "Borrowed £200 till payday, ended up owing loads more, kept borrowing to pay it back." What happened? | He was trapped by an expensive payday loan | He won money in a prize draw on the telly | He got a free loan from a charity | His savings grew by compound interest
test: Your mate whispers "Bailiffs can take anything, even your bed and your telly!" Put him right. | They follow strict rules and can't take essentials | He is right, they can take anything at all, even the beds | Bailiffs can only take children's toys | Bailiffs come without any warning ever
test: Two loans for the same car: one adds £5 a year for every £100 borrowed, the other £15. Which costs less in total? | The one that adds £5 | The one that adds £15 | They cost exactly the same | The one that adds more is always cheaper
test: Your sister lent her friend £5 for sweets. It's been a month, nothing back, and now they're not talking. What lesson is this? | Lending to friends can cause arguments if not repaid | Friends always pay back twice as much if you wait long enough | Never share anything with anyone ever | She should charge her friend interest
test: Mum, quietly: "We'll be fine, the debt people are helping us." What should you do? | Trust that the grown-ups are sorting it | Give all your pocket money to the bank to help | Ask the neighbours to lend you money | Worry in secret every night
test: Grandad: "Savings and debts grow the same way, kid." What does he mean? | Interest builds up on both over time | Debts shrink while savings grow | Savings grow only if you have no debts | Interest is only paid on savings
test: Dad: "If we'd saved up first we'd have paid £800 less for this car." Why? | Buying with a loan adds interest to the price | Cars are always cheaper if you pay for them later | Saved money earns a discount at the garage | The loan company gave him a refund
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the consequence of remitting only the minimum payment upon a credit card balance? | The debt lasts a long time, as little more than the interest is discharged | The balance is extinguished within a single month of the first payment | No interest whatsoever accrues upon the balance | The lender is obliged to waive the remainder
exam: Identify the principal function of a credit score. | To indicate the reliability with which an individual has discharged past debts | To record the aggregate savings held by an individual in all accounts across the country | To state the salary received by an individual | To determine the council tax payable by a household
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning the enforcement of debts by bailiffs. | Enforcement occurs only after a court decision, subject to strict rules | Enforcement may occur at any hour and without any prior warning whatsoever | Bailiffs are empowered to seize essential household items | Bailiffs act solely on the instruction of neighbours
exam: Ascertain the most prudent course of action for a household in receipt of a final demand which it is unable to settle. | To seek free advice from a debt charity without delay | To disregard the correspondence until further notice is received | To obtain a payday loan of the highest available sum | To conceal the correspondence from all other persons
